+/// How many rows one page of the [`high_water_marks`] walk asks for — **this reader's
+/// constant**, never a number a stored record chooses, the rule [`SEGMENT_PAGE_LIMIT`] states
+/// for the resolver's own range read (and the same width, over rows of the same class).
+///
+/// What a page bounds, and what it does not, is the split stated in full below ("WHAT IS
+/// BOUNDED HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT"). The **rows** one round trip materialises are bounded
+/// here, and this walk retains none of them — it carries one [`InodeId`], so its own residency
+/// is a page at a time however large the namespace is. The **bytes** those rows arrive as are
+/// the *seam's* bound and not this walk's: [`MetadataStore::scan_page`] hands back `Bytes`
+/// already in the caller's heap, a backend's native value ceiling is inherited and surfaces as
+/// `Err` (`crates/traits/src/lib.rs:995-999`), and closing that gap is tracked as
+/// getwyrd/wyrd#674. A **conforming** record is at most [`MAX_VALUE_BYTES`], so a page of
+/// conforming rows costs ~12 MB; a stored value past that ceiling is one no conforming writer
+/// produced and the tightest backend in play would have refused, and this walk neither decodes
+/// nor retains it ([`read_inode_value`]) — which is not a claim that no memory was spent on
+/// one.
+const RECOVERY_PAGE: usize = 128;
+
+/// What one `inode:` row's **value** is to [`high_water_marks`] — an attribution, and nothing
+/// else: the row's id comes from its *key*, so the value is read only to tell an operator
+/// which records are damaged.
+#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+enum InodeValueReading {
+    /// The record decodes. Nothing is owed to anyone.
+    Readable,
+    /// Past [`MAX_VALUE_BYTES`] — neither decoded nor retained.
+    OverCeiling { bytes: usize },
+    /// Within the ceiling, but the decoder refused it.
+    Undecodable { detail: String },
+}
+
+/// Read one `inode:` row's value far enough to say whether it is damaged, and how — never
+/// far enough to let it end the walk.
+///
+/// The ceiling is checked **before** the decode, and strictly (`>`): a value of exactly
+/// [`MAX_VALUE_BYTES`] is the largest a conforming writer may store, so it is a record to
+/// decode, not damage to report. Past it, the row is one no conforming writer produced and
+/// the tightest backend in play would have refused to store
+/// (`crates/traits/src/lib.rs:995-999`), so it is neither decoded nor retained — the rule the
+/// resolver's own range read already applies to a `seg:` row one screen below
+/// ([`read_group_range`], `SegmentValueOverCeiling`).
+///
+/// Separated from the walk so the boundary is a *unit* a test can stand on: the difference
+/// between `>` and `>=` here is one conforming record's attribution, which no end-to-end
+/// assertion over a store distinguishes.
+fn read_inode_value(value: &[u8]) -> InodeValueReading {
+    if value.len() > MAX_VALUE_BYTES {
+        return InodeValueReading::OverCeiling { bytes: value.len() };
+    }
+    match decode::<InodeRecord>(value) {
+        Ok(_) => InodeValueReading::Readable,
+        Err(err) => InodeValueReading::Undecodable {
+            detail: err.to_string(),
+        },
+    }
 }
 
