+/// The high-water mark of the persisted **inode** allocator: the largest inode id any
+/// `inode:` key names. An empty store yields `0`, so allocation starts at 1 — unchanged.
+///
+/// A gateway allocates inodes from the shared, persisted `meta:next_inode` counter
+/// (`server::cli::alloc_inode`); over a store that counter has never seen — a **legacy** store
+/// an older single-process gateway wrote from an in-memory counter — it must resume *above*
+/// everything already on disk. Otherwise a new-key PUT reuses a committed inode id, a bogus
+/// "concurrent writer won" conflict, because [`create`] is `require_absent` on the inode key
+/// (issue #364 durability finding 1). This walk supplies the mark `Gateway::recover` seeds
+/// that counter from (`crates/server/src/lib.rs:139-141`).
+///
+/// # It is total over what is stored, and bounded by what it reads
+///
+/// `Gateway::recover` runs this **before the gateway serves anything**, so an `Err` here
+/// spends every *healthy* object's availability on one record — a permanent failure mode
+/// nothing but manual repair exits, which is the cost C-1 does not accept
+/// (`docs/principles.md` §5). Two properties keep that from happening, and both are
+/// load-bearing rather than defensive:
+///
+/// * **The mark comes from the key, so damage cannot lower it.** `inode:<id>` names the id;
+///   the value beside it is never consulted for it. A value that will not decode — #648
+///   enforces the segmented root's structural invariants at decode (ADR-0045), which widens
+///   the set of values that can fail — is therefore attributed and stepped over, never
+///   `?`-propagated. Containing one record's damage never under-approximates the floor the
+///   allocator then trusts, which is the other half of the rule: the two legal answers for a
+///   record a recovery cannot read are *fail closed for that record* or *contribute its true
+///   floor*, never zero.
+/// * **The walk is paged** ([`for_each_page`]), never [`MetadataStore::scan`]: a store merely
+///   *larger* than `SCAN_CAP` stops a gateway from starting exactly as effectively as a
+///   corrupt record does, so a containment rule that held for one and not the other would not
+///   be a containment rule (`crates/traits/src/lib.rs:286`, `:1086-1087`).
+///
+/// Every row the walk cannot fully account for is attributed on [`RECOVERY_AUDIT`] with the
+/// key that names it: a silent skip is the one answer a recovery path must never give, and
+/// the attribution is the repair obligation an operator works from.
+///
+/// # There is no chunk-id mark here, deliberately
+///
+/// This walk used to compute one beside the inode mark (plus the `pending:` and `orphan:`
+/// walks that fed it). It had no consumer: `fdd34f1` — "server: make gateway id allocation
+/// safe for active-active gateways" (#487) — replaced the sequential in-process chunk-id
+/// counter that once resumed from it with a coordination-free scheme whose per-process random
+/// epoch puts every minted id at or above `2^127` (`crates/server/src/lib.rs:255-257`,
+/// `:269-275`), and in the same commit rewrote `Gateway::recover` to discard the floor.
+/// The cluster path's minter never needed it either: it derives `(inode << 64) | seq`
+/// (`server::cli::chunk_id_minter`, `crates/server/src/cli.rs:1861-1868`) and resumes from
+/// the same `meta:next_inode` counter the inode mark above seeds, so every id it mints is at
+/// or above `2^64`. Nothing in this tree mints into the `< 2^64` space that floor guarded.
+/// Computing it more carefully is not how a mark with no reader earns its safety claim
+/// (issue #652); removing it is, and it takes two unbounded startup `scan`s with it.
+pub async fn high_water_marks(store: &impl MetadataStore) -> Result<InodeId> {
+    let mut max_inode: InodeId = 0;
+    for_each_page(store, b"inode:", RECOVERY_PAGE, |key, value| {
+        let Some(id) = parse_inode_key(&key) else {
+            // A key under `inode:` that this module's own grammar refuses. No id can be
+            // recovered from it, so it is the one row that contributes nothing — attributed
+            // rather than skipped, because "nothing to contribute" and "nobody looked" must
+            // not be the same observation to an operator.
+            tracing::warn!(
+                target: RECOVERY_AUDIT,
+                action = "inode-key-unparsable",
+                key = %String::from_utf8_lossy(&key),
+                "startup id recovery met a key under `inode:` its grammar cannot parse: no \
+                 id is recoverable from it, and the walk continues",
+            );
+            return;
+        };
+        // Taken BEFORE the value is looked at, so nothing the value does can cost the key
+        // its id.
+        max_inode = max_inode.max(id);
+        match read_inode_value(&value) {
+            InodeValueReading::Readable => {}
+            InodeValueReading::OverCeiling { bytes } => tracing::warn!(
+                target: RECOVERY_AUDIT,
+                action = "inode-record-over-ceiling",
+                key = %String::from_utf8_lossy(&key),
+                bytes,
+                ceiling = MAX_VALUE_BYTES,
+                "startup id recovery met an inode record past the value ceiling and did not \
+                 decode it; its id is still recovered from its key and the walk continues",
+            ),
+            InodeValueReading::Undecodable { detail } => tracing::warn!(
+                target: RECOVERY_AUDIT,
+                action = "inode-record-undecodable",
+                key = %String::from_utf8_lossy(&key),
+                detail,
+                "startup id recovery could not decode an inode record; its id is still \
+                 recovered from its key and the walk continues — the gateway must not refuse \
+                 to start over one damaged record",
+            ),
         }
-    }
-    Ok((max_inode, max_chunk))
+    })
+    .await?;
+    Ok(max_inode)
 }
 
